{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"self-edit-document-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Self-edit a document track","description":"Takes a long non-fiction document through gated self-editing passes, from purpose and structure to paragraphs, lines, consistency and a final proof, pausing for approval at each.","category":"editing","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["review","verify"],"role":["writer","individual","student","manager"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["document","text"],"output":["report","rewrite","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["self-editing","revision-passes","structural-edit","line-edit","consistency-check"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["edit-for-structure","tighten-prose","proofread-text","build-style-sheet"],"personas":["editor"]},"args":[{"name":"document","description":"The full document (report, guide, proposal, thesis chapter, long article). For very long documents, paste the outline plus the first section and add sections when a step asks.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"purpose_and_reader","description":"Who will read it, what they should know or do afterwards, any length limit or style guide, and the deadline.","type":"text","required":true}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Structure plan","Paragraph pass","Line pass","Style sheet","Final proof"]},"steps":[{"id":"purpose-structure","file":"steps/01-purpose-structure.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"self-edit/01-structure-plan.md"},{"id":"paragraphs","file":"steps/02-paragraphs.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"self-edit/02-paragraph-pass.md"},{"id":"lines","file":"steps/03-lines.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"self-edit/03-line-pass.md"},{"id":"consistency","file":"steps/04-consistency.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","artifact":"self-edit/04-style-sheet.md"},{"id":"final-proof","file":"steps/05-final-proof.md","stage":"verify","gate":"none","artifact":"self-edit/05-final-proof.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Edits a long document the way professional editors sequence the work: big to small, one kind of attention per pass. Polishing sentences in a section that will be cut wastes the most time, and proofreading before the wording settles means proofreading twice.\n\n<purpose_and_reader>\n{{purpose_and_reader}}\n</purpose_and_reader>\n\n<document>\n{{document}}\n</document>\n\nRules for every step: the document belongs to the writer, so show changes and reasons and let them decide; never change facts, figures, names or quotations, and raise doubts as questions; do not re-open a decision approved in an earlier step without flagging it; work only from text the writer has pasted, and say \"not seen yet\" for missing sections. If the writer wants to skip a pass, say in one line what that risks, then do as they ask.","steps":[{"id":"purpose-structure","file":"steps/01-purpose-structure.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"self-edit/01-structure-plan.md","text":"# Step 1: Purpose and structure\n\n1. If the reader or purpose is unclear, ask and stop.\n2. State the document's main point in one or two sentences. If you cannot, that is the first problem: say what competing points you found.\n3. Reverse-outline it: one line per section saying what it does for the reader.\n4. Flag sections that are out of order, repeat each other, do not serve the purpose, or are missing (for example no recommendation, no next steps, background with no conclusion).\n5. Check the opening: can the reader learn the main point and why it matters from the first paragraph or summary?\n6. Propose a revised outline with moves, cuts, merges and additions, each with a reason, and estimate the length change against any limit.\n\nWrite sections Main point, Reverse outline, Problems, Proposed outline. Stop and wait for approval before touching paragraphs."},{"id":"paragraphs","file":"steps/02-paragraphs.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"self-edit/02-paragraph-pass.md","text":"# Step 2: Paragraphs\n\nApply the approved outline, then edit at paragraph level.\n\n1. Show the restructured document, marking moved, merged and new sections. Where new content is needed, insert [WRITER TO ADD: what is needed] instead of inventing it.\n2. Check that each paragraph has one job and a first sentence that says what it is.\n3. Fix transitions between paragraphs and sections so the reader always knows why the next part follows.\n4. Split overloaded paragraphs; merge fragments that make one point.\n5. List each change in a short table: location, change, reason.\n\nStop for approval before line editing."},{"id":"lines","file":"steps/03-lines.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"self-edit/03-line-pass.md","text":"# Step 3: Lines\n\n1. Edit sentences for clarity and economy: cut filler and throat-clearing, prefer concrete verbs to nominalisations, break sentences that carry more than one main idea, put the important information at the end of the sentence.\n2. Keep the writer's voice and the register the reader expects.\n3. Show the edited text with changes marked, a section at a time if long, and name the two or three habits behind most edits so the writer can fix them next time.\n4. Report the word count before and after, against any limit.\n\nStop for approval before the consistency pass."},{"id":"consistency","file":"steps/04-consistency.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","artifact":"self-edit/04-style-sheet.md","text":"# Step 4: Consistency\n\n1. Build a short style sheet from the approved text: spelling variety, terms and their one chosen form, capitalisation, numbers and units, dates, abbreviations (defined at first use), list and heading style, and any style guide named in the brief.\n2. List every inconsistency with its locations and the form you propose.\n3. Check internal references: section numbers, \"see above\" and \"as noted\" pointers, figure and table mentions, and that totals and repeated figures match each other.\n4. Apply only the approved fixes.\n\nStop for approval of the style sheet and fixes."},{"id":"final-proof","file":"steps/05-final-proof.md","stage":"verify","gate":"none","artifact":"self-edit/05-final-proof.md","text":"# Step 5: Final proof\n\n1. Proofread the final text for typos, doubled or missing words, agreement, punctuation and apostrophes, without restyling.\n2. Check headings, list numbering and any placeholder text left behind ([WRITER TO ADD], TK, XX, comments).\n3. Output the clean final text, a table of proof corrections, and a short release checklist of what only the writer can check (facts, links, names, approvals, layout)."}]}
